= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Work over the <complex numbers>. The chain $S_0\subset S_1\subset\cdots\subset S_n$ has <multiplicity-free restriction>: each <irreducible representation> restricts to a <direct sum> of pairwise inequivalent <irreducible representations>. This structural fact can be proved before identifying the branching diagram. Indeed, the permitted <Olshanskii centralizer lemma> makes $C_{\mathbb C S_r}(\mathbb C S_{r-1})$ commutative, since it is generated by the center at the previous level and the commuting element $X_r$. In each irreducible block this is the endomorphism algebra of the restricted module; a repeated summand would give a noncommutative <matrix algebra> factor. Thus no identification of the branching graph with <Young diagrams> is being assumed here.

Successively restricting an <irreducible representation> $V$ gives one-dimensional spaces $V_T$ indexed by paths of <irreducible representations> from the trivial $S_0$-module to $V$. Choosing one nonzero vector in each $V_T$ gives a <Gelfand–Tsetlin basis>. Define the <Gelfand–Tsetlin algebra> as the subalgebra of $\mathbb C S_n$ acting diagonally in all these bases. The <Artin–Wedderburn theorem> identifies
$$
\mathbb C S_n\cong\bigoplus_{V\in\operatorname{Irr}(S_n)}\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(V).
$$
Under this identification the <Gelfand–Tsetlin algebra> is the direct sum of the full diagonal <matrix algebras> in the indicated bases, so it is commutative.

To see that it is actually available inside the <group algebra>, let $e_\alpha^{(r)}$ be the central <primitive idempotent> selecting the <irreducible representation> $\alpha$ of $S_r$. For a path $T=(\alpha_0,\ldots,\alpha_n)$ the product
$$
P_T=\prod_{r=0}^n e_{\alpha_r}^{(r)}
$$
is the projection onto $V_T$ in its final <irreducible representation> and is zero in every other final block. These products commute: a center at a higher level commutes with every element at a lower level. The $P_T$ are precisely the diagonal <matrix units>, and therefore span the proposed algebra.

If an element of the <group algebra> commutes with every $P_T$, its matrix preserves every $V_T$ and is diagonal in every block. It already belongs to the <Gelfand–Tsetlin algebra>. Thus
$$
\boxed{C_{\mathbb C S_n}(GZ_n)=GZ_n,}
$$
which proves that it is a <maximal commutative subalgebra>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The projections $P_T$ in the <Gelfand–Tsetlin algebra> satisfy
$$
P_TP_R=\delta_{TR}P_T,\qquad \sum_TP_T=1.
$$
Consequently the map sending a diagonal element to its scalar on each <Gelfand–Tsetlin basis> vector is a <ring isomorphism>
$$
\boxed{GZ_n\cong\prod_T\mathbb C.}
$$
Each factor is a <simple module> over this algebra, and the algebra is their <direct sum> as a module over itself. This proves that $GZ_n$ is a <semisimple algebra>. The argument uses its explicit diagonal structure; being a subalgebra of a <semisimple algebra> by itself would not suffice.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Young–Jucys–Murphy elements> are
$$
X_1=0,\qquad X_r=\sum_{a=1}^{r-1}(a\ r)\quad(r\geq2),
$$
with products of <permutations> composed from right to left. Fix $i<j$. In $[X_i,X_j]$, all pairs of disjoint <transpositions> commute. The only terms left, for each $a<i$, are
$$
[(a\ i),(i\ j)]+[(a\ i),(a\ j)]=0.
$$
Indeed $(a\ i)(i\ j)=(a\ j)(a\ i)$ and $(i\ j)(a\ i)=(a\ i)(a\ j)$. Summing directly proves
$$
\boxed{[X_i,X_j]=0\qquad(1\leq i,j\leq n).}
$$
Thus all the <Young–Jucys–Murphy elements> commute.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <primitive idempotent> products used above show that the <Gelfand–Tsetlin algebra> is generated by the centers at all levels: those products give every diagonal <matrix unit>, whereas each center acts diagonally. Hence
$$
\boxed{GZ_n=\operatorname{alg}(Z_0,Z_1,\ldots,Z_n).}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the second generation assertion use the permitted <Olshanskii centralizer lemma>, in the precise form
$$
C_{\mathbb C S_r}(\mathbb C S_{r-1})=\operatorname{alg}(Z_{r-1},X_r).
$$
In particular $Z_r\subseteq\operatorname{alg}(Z_{r-1},X_r)$. Induction puts every $Z_r$ in $\operatorname{alg}(1,X_1,\ldots,X_r)$. Conversely, if $A_r$ is the sum of all <transpositions> in $S_r$, then $A_r\in Z_r$ and $X_r=A_r-A_{r-1}\in GZ_n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{GZ_n=\operatorname{alg}(1,X_1,\ldots,X_n).}
$$
Here generation is unital; the zero generator $X_1$ causes no problem.

